 currently hold Ace of Clubs and 9 of Clubs.  \n- The board shows: 8♣, 10♥, 9♥, 7♦.  \n- You have a **pair of 9s** (with your hole card), and the board has 8, 10, 7 — which gives a possible straight draw (7-8-9-10).  \n- You also have four cards to a flush (you hold A♣ and 9♣, and there's 8♣ on the board) — so you have a **flush draw** with one more club needed.  \n- Additionally, with A-9-8-7 and the 10 on board, an open-ended straight draw is possible: hitting a **6** or **J** completes a straight (6-7-8-9-10 or 7-8-9-10-J).  \n- So you have strong draws: **flush draw**, **open-ended straight draw**, and **a pair of 9s**. This gives you many outs (~15+ outs), making this a very strong drawing hand.  \n- Your opponent checked behind on the flop and called your small 100-chip raise on the turn, suggesting a medium-strength hand or possibly just waiting to see the river. They may have top pair (like A-10, K-10), middle pair, or a weaker draw.  \n- Since the pot is building and you’re in position (implied by acting first in prior rounds), betting here can either build value or force folds from weaker hands.\n\nOptions:\n- **Fold (-1)**: Not justified — you have strong equity.\n- **Check (0)**: Allows free showdown; lets opponent hit lucky river for free. Lowers value extraction.\n- **Raise >100**: Builds the pot while you have high equity. Even if called, you still have many outs.\n- **All-in (-2)**: Overkill unless you suspect weakness; risks too much given stack size (19,800 chips) and only 4th hand of 20. Better to keep flexibility.\n- **Raise 100**: Minimal bet increase — safe but may not extract enough value.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a strong semi-bluff opportunity. With a pair, flush draw, and straight draw, your hand has high equity (~50%+ against many made hands). Raising around 100–300 would be ideal, but since 100 is the minimum raise allowed above current bet, and larger raises are valid, going slightly bigger (e.g., 300) could maximize value and fold equity. However, the valid actions include *any value above 100*, so we can choose a moderate raise. But note: raising only 100 may look weak or be interpreted as probing. A larger bet (e.g., 500) builds the pot appropriately and pressures marginal hands. Given that stacks are deep (19,800), a 500-chip bet is only ~2.5% of stack — very sustainable.\n\nBut: since your last action was a 100 raise and opponent called, repeating a small raise may not help. Instead, a stronger bet like **500** increases pressure and extracts more from worse draws or marginal pairs.\n\nAnswer: 500"}
