e above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nYou hold A♣ 9♣. The board is: 8♣, 10♥, 9♥, 7♦, 6♥.  \nLet’s analyze your hand and possible combinations:\n\n- You have **One Pair**: 9♣ + 9♥ = a pair of 9s.\n- There is an open-ended straight draw on the board: 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 — anyone holding a J completes a straight (6-7-8-9-10-J).\n- There is also a flush draw in clubs: you have A♣ and the board has 8♣, so two club cards on the board. That gives you four clubs across your hand and the board (A♣, 8♣, plus possibly others). Wait — let's recount:\n  - Your hand: A♣, 9♣\n  - Board: 8♣, 10♥, 9♥, 7♦, 6♥ → only one club on the board: 8♣.\n  - So total clubs in your hand + board: A♣, 9♣, 8♣ → three clubs. Not enough for a flush. So no flush.\n\nWait — correction: board has only **one club** (8♣), so with your two club cards, that’s three total — not a flush. So you do **not** have a flush.\n\nBut note: the board has 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 — that’s four consecutive ranks: 6, 7, 8, 9, 10. With five distinct ranks from 6 to 10, this forms a **straight**: 6-7-8-9-10. This is a **board straight**.\n\nImportant: The best five-card hand using the board is **6-7-8-9-10**, which is a straight.\n\nSince both players can use the board, **the board itself makes a straight**. Therefore, unless someone has a higher straight (J-10-9-8-7 → J high) or a full house/flush/etc., the pot will likely be split if no one improves.\n\nBut wait: 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 — yes, that's five consecutive cards. So **the board plays** as a straight.\n\nNow, does your hand improve upon the board?\n\nYour hole cards: A♣, 9♣.\n\nYou use the 9♥ and your 9♣ to make a **pair of 9s**, but the board already includes 9♥, so even without your card, 9 is on the board.\n\nBut the board straight is 6-7-8-9-10 — all distinct. So the highest card in the straight is 10. It's a **ten-high straight**.\n\nCan you beat that?\n\nNo — you don’t have a J, so you can't make J-10-9-8-7 (jack-high straight). You only have A and 9, neither of which play in improving the straight.\n\nSo your best five-card hand is the **board straight**: 6-7-8-9-10.\n\nSame as what any other player would get if they just play the board.\n\nBut you have a **pair of 9s** in your hand — however, the straight outranks a pair, so your pair doesn’t matter — your hand strength is still the straight.\n\nHowever, here's the catch: **if your opponent does not have a Jack**, then they cannot complete a higher straight either. But if they have a Jack, they have J-10-9-8-7 = jack-high straight, which beats your ten-high straight.\n\nAlso, someone with J-x has a straight too — and it's higher.\n\nMoreover, could someone have a flush? Let's check suits:\n\n- Clubs: only three on the table including yours — A♣, 9♣, 8♣ — so max three clubs total. No flush possible for you.\n- Hearts: 10♥, 9♥, 6♥ — three hearts on board. If opponent has two hearts, they could have a flush.\n- Diamonds: only 7♦ — one diamond.\n- Spades: none visible.\n\nSo flushes are possible only if someone has two hearts.\n\nBut more importantly: **the nuts (best hand)** here is **Jack of any suit**, giving J-10-9-8-7 = straight, jack-high.\n\nSecond best: Ten with a Jack? No — the key card is Jack.\n\nSo if opponent has **any Jack**, they beat you.\n\nIf they have **no Jack**, then the best they can do is tie you with the board straight.\n\nBut you have **a pair of 9s** in hand — does that help? No, because the straight is already made on the board. Even if they have no pair, the straight is still the same.\n\nSo: **if opponent does not have a Jack, the pot is split**.\n\n**If opponent has a Jack, they win**.\n\nNow, consider betting history:\n\n- Round 0 (pre-flop): you checked, opponent checked.\n- Round 1 (flop): you bet 100, opponent called (0 implies call since bet was 100).\n- Round 2 (turn): you raised 500, opponent called.\n\nThis shows aggression from you, and passive but calling behavior from opponent. They’ve stayed in through two bets, suggesting they have something.\n\nGiven the river is now dealt (6♥), completing the 6-7-8-9-10 straight, and knowing that only a Jack beats this, we must assess: did the opponent call two bets without a Jack?\n\nPossibilities:\n- They have a Jack → they have a better straight → they will likely bet or raise.\n- They have nothing (e.g. weak hand) → but they called two bets? Unlikely unless bluffing or drawing.\n- They had a draw: like J-anything, or maybe 5-x for the lower end? But 5-6-7-8-9 is also a nine-high straight — weaker than ten-high. So no.\n- Or they had two pair? Possible earlier — e.g. 10-6, or 8-6, but unlikely given they didn’t raise.\n\nBut most importantly: **you cannot beat any Jack**.\n\nAnd if they don’t have a Jack, **you tie**.\n\nSo there is **no hand you beat** except pure air (like A-K who missed everything). But such hands likely would have folded to your turn bet.\n\nTherefore, your best outcome is a **split pot** (if they don’t have Jack), worst is **losing** (if they do).\n\nNow, current action options: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100, or any raise >100]\n\nSince you're out of position and the board is scary (straight completed), and you can't beat a Jack, **checking is safest**.\n\nIf you bet or raise, only worse hands fold (but few exist), and better hands (with Jack) call or raise.\n\nThus, **bluff-raising doesn’t work** — no one folds a Jack.\n\n**Value-betting doesn’t work** — no worse hand calls (since any calling hand likely has a Jack or is strong).\n\nEven a hand like 10-9 has the same straight — they’d call, but it's a tie.\n\nBut again, you can't extract value from worse hands because there are essentially no worse hands that will call.\n\nHence, **checking is optimal** to avoid losing more chips when behind, and potentially inducing a bluff if opponent thinks they’re beaten.\n\nBut opponent may also check back with weak holdings.\n\nAlternatively, small bet might get called by weaker pairs — but again, straight is on board, so any rational player knows they need at least a Jack to win.\n\nBut some players may overvalue top pair (like A-10) and call with non-straight hands — but A-10 has the 10, so they have the straight too — same as you.\n\nAlmost every reasonable hand that called your previous bets now has at least the same straight.\n\nSo **no meaningful fold equity or value exists**.\n\nThus, checking to see showdown is best.\n\nReason:  \nThe board completes a ten-high straight, which is your best hand. Any Jack beats you. Opponent has shown willingness to call, suggesting strength. Since you cannot beat a Jack and likely tie or lose, betting risks more chips with no positive expectation. Checking minimizes loss and proceeds to showdown safely.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
